::GEO_U05_L05_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.1
Question:
In construction task 1, which construction locates the circumcenter of a triangle?
{
=A. Construct the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle sides.#The circumcenter is where the perpendicular bisectors meet, and it is the center of the circumscribed circle.
~B. Construct only one median of the triangle.#One median does not locate the center of the circumscribed circle.
~C. Draw any chord of the circle.#A random chord does not determine the circumcenter.
~D. Bisect only one angle of the triangle.#Angle bisectors locate the incenter, not the circumcenter.
}

::GEO_U05_L05_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.1
Question:
In construction task 2, which construction locates the incenter of a triangle?
{
~A. Construct the perpendicular bisectors of the sides.#Perpendicular bisectors locate the circumcenter, not the incenter.
=B. Construct the angle bisectors of the triangle.#The incenter is the intersection of the angle bisectors and is the center of the inscribed circle.
~C. Connect each vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.#Medians locate the centroid, not the incenter.
~D. Draw a tangent from an external point.#A tangent construction does not locate a triangle incenter.
}

::GEO_U05_L05_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.2
Question:
For construction task 3, a regular hexagon is inscribed in a circle. Which fact supports the construction?
{
~A. The diameter is used as each side of the hexagon.#The diameter would be too long to form six equal sides around the circle.
~B. Any six points on the circle form a regular hexagon.#The points must be equally spaced, not just on the circle.
=C. The radius can be stepped around the circle six times as equal chords.#In a regular hexagon inscribed in a circle, each side length equals the radius.
~D. A tangent line gives each side of the hexagon.#Tangents touch the circle but do not form the inscribed sides.
}

::GEO_U05_L05_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.3
Question:
In construction task 4, a tangent must be drawn from external point P to a circle. Which relationship must the final tangent segment satisfy?
{
~A. It must pass through the center of the circle.#A line through the center and the point on the circle is a radius line, not a tangent.
~B. It must intersect the circle at two points.#A line intersecting a circle at two points is a secant.
~C. It must have the same length as the diameter.#Tangent length is not defined by matching the diameter.
=D. It must be perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency.#A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of tangency.
}

::GEO_U05_L05_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.2
Question:
In construction task 5, why must vertices of an inscribed regular polygon be equally spaced on the circle?
{
=A. Equal spacing creates congruent central angles and congruent sides.#A regular polygon has equal sides and angles, which comes from equal spacing around the circle.
~B. Equal spacing makes the polygon tangent to the circle.#The polygon is inscribed, so its vertices lie on the circle; it is not tangent to the circle.
~C. Equal spacing changes the radius of the circle.#The radius remains fixed during the construction.
~D. Equal spacing is only needed for triangles.#Regular polygons with more sides also require equal spacing.
}

